language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ Jobs go through the machines in the same order (machine 1 to machine M); a job starts on a machine when the machine is free and the job has finished on the previous machine. Find the job sequence that minimises the makespan, the time when all jobs have been processed.
given process_time_dim1, process_time_dim2 : int(1..)
$ process_time[j,m] is the time needed to process job j on machine m
given process_time : matrix indexed by [int(1..process_time_dim1), int(1..process_time_dim2)] of int(0..)
$ seq[k] is the job processed in position k of the sequence
find seq : matrix indexed by [int(1..process_time_dim1)] of int(1..process_time_dim1)
$ C[k,m] is the time at which the job in position k finishes on machine m
find C : matrix indexed by [int(1..process_time_dim1), int(1..process_time_dim2)] of int(0..sum(flatten(process_time)))
find makespan : int(0..sum(flatten(process_time)))

minimising makespan
such that
$ the sequence is a permutation of the jobs
allDiff(seq),
$ the first job of the sequence finishes on the first machine after its processing time
C[1,1] = process_time[seq[1],1],
$ the first job of the sequence goes through the machines one after the other
forAll m : int(2..process_time_dim2) . C[1,m] = C[1,m-1] + process_time[seq[1],m],
$ on the first machine the jobs follow each other in sequence order
forAll k : int(2..process_time_dim1) . C[k,1] = C[k-1,1] + process_time[seq[k],1],
$ a job starts on a machine only after the previous job has left that machine and it has finished on the previous machine
forAll k : int(2..process_time_dim1) . forAll m : int(2..process_time_dim2) . C[k,m] = max(C[k-1,m], C[k,m-1]) + process_time[seq[k],m],
$ the makespan is the finish time of the last job on the last machine
makespan = C[process_time_dim1, process_time_dim2]
